#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147293 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7887c216-958f-5dfe-8889-b4c3033fbafe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6624" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n1. $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n2. $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n3. $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" :question/worked-solution "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2$, $b=6$, $c=-5$, and $f(x)=0$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2$. Instead, it is multiplied by an $x$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "08304289-e9fc-5914-a6e7-187ea5a40031" :question/math-academy-id "e-6625" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0$." :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 8t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n3λ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 8λt^{λ} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n3λ^{2} + 5λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(3λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= c_{1}t^{-2} + c_{2}t^{1/3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{t^{2}} + c_{2}t^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147295 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10cb8c3a-0764-5082-b431-7f97b8bc5782" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "d86f4d20-8ebe-52ea-bf49-f0cdcd0a2600" :question/math-academy-id "e-6626" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2}$,\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c_2 = -c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|=-3\\frac{c_{1}}{x^{4}} + 2c_{2}x$.\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n$5|=-3c_{1} + 2c_{2}; 5|=-3c_{1} + 2(-c_{1}); 5|=-5c_{1}; c_{1}|=-1⇒c_{2} = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6523c3be-22c9-5b57-985f-1af4fa4a3cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Instances of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b6a3398-cfaa-5f0d-85d9-7071da4bd6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy^{″} + 4xy^{′} + 7y = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146810} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3fe262-e221-5601-a0a9-649cf6d90905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6xy^{′} + 9x^{2}y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146811} {:answer/id #uuid "ad87c5ab-3ea5-5a77-92b7-70a97008b03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 2x^{2}y^{′} + 4y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146812} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab23060-ad14-5261-b4a3-0fda78161658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146813} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab5e81d-9fb8-52c0-8987-9f4a03937761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + 6y^{′} + 9y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab23060-ad14-5261-b4a3-0fda78161658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + 2xy^{′} + 4y = \\frac{2}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146813} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f438a8f-b8f8-5450-bf9f-bfc845118b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146809}] :question/id #uuid "b7e398da-afcf-578c-b016-2a95047aca8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64714" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2cb1a475-4f73-531d-b14c-37000cfc37ab" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Instance of the Cauchy-Euler Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897dfbcc-99f3-51b4-9d08-2a67866b90e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146834} {:answer/id #uuid "923d63a8-b33c-5aa6-bed3-9bef12b810bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146835} {:answer/id #uuid "b84dcc21-9de4-5972-bd52-95bb0fdd7c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{-3} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146836} {:answer/id #uuid "18850531-390c-5ee4-896e-805f4ffa9a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146837} {:answer/id #uuid "6c684eb2-61ac-50f4-93cb-a2cd438b388e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18850531-390c-5ee4-896e-805f4ffa9a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = c_{1}t^{2} + c_{2}t^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146837} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce2d2a5-35d9-5e66-8012-f8d04e4a461a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146833}] :question/id #uuid "f0f78c40-43f4-5bba-8222-db99c2fa4af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64803" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 2y = 0, t > 0$."}]}
